1
00:00:00,275 --> 00:00:05,188
December 10th, 2025, a few seconds before two o'clock in the afternoon.

2
00:00:05,188 --> 00:00:09,462
In a data center in Carteret, New Jersey, Nasdaq's computers are processing

3
00:00:09,462 --> 00:00:11,588
a stream of messages from traders:

4
00:00:11,588 --> 00:00:15,625
orders to buy, orders to sell, changes, cancellations.

5
00:00:15,625 --> 00:00:19,575
At exactly two o'clock, the Federal Reserve will announce its decision on

6
00:00:19,575 --> 00:00:22,288
interest rates, and the machines know it.

7
00:00:22,288 --> 00:00:26,125
In the last second, they start pulling their orders out of the book.

8
00:00:26,125 --> 00:00:27,875
Half a second before two:

9
00:00:27,875 --> 00:00:32,500
sixteen thousand cancellations in a tenth of a second, across more than fourteen

10
00:00:32,500 --> 00:00:34,000
hundred stocks.

11
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:37,542
Nobody wants to be standing in the book when the news lands.

12
00:00:37,542 --> 00:00:38,579
Two o'clock.

13
00:00:38,579 --> 00:00:40,154
A quarter-point cut.

14
00:00:40,154 --> 00:00:44,254
Within a second the machines are back, quoting and trading furiously:

15
00:00:44,254 --> 00:00:48,417
297 thousand messages in the next three seconds.

16
00:00:48,417 --> 00:00:52,979
Nearly every one was generated by a computer, most of them within microseconds

17
00:00:52,979 --> 00:00:56,617
of the computer seeing something change.

18
00:00:56,887 --> 00:00:58,562
Zoom out to the whole day.

19
00:00:58,562 --> 00:01:00,925
The opening bell at 9:30:

20
00:01:00,925 --> 00:01:04,512
more than seven hundred thousand messages in a single second.

21
00:01:04,512 --> 00:01:05,987
The Fed at two.

22
00:01:05,987 --> 00:01:09,975
And the busiest second of the regular session came forty minutes later, during

23
00:01:09,975 --> 00:01:12,112
the Fed chair's press conference:

24
00:01:12,112 --> 00:01:15,637
933 thousand messages in one second.

25
00:01:15,637 --> 00:01:18,562
The close at four is busier still.

26
00:01:18,562 --> 00:01:25,762
In total, Nasdaq's feed carried 846.8 million messages that day, 27 gigabytes,

27
00:01:25,762 --> 00:01:30,912
from one exchange among more than a dozen in the United States.

28
00:01:31,021 --> 00:01:34,871
The firms that thrive in this torrent are among the most profitable businesses

29
00:01:34,871 --> 00:01:36,021
in the world.

30
00:01:36,021 --> 00:01:40,421
Jane Street trades on more than two hundred venues in forty-five countries,

31
00:01:40,421 --> 00:01:46,258
and reportedly made 39.6 billion dollars of trading revenue in 2025.

32
00:01:46,258 --> 00:01:51,046
Hudson River Trading says it trades on nearly all the world's electronic markets.

33
00:01:51,046 --> 00:01:52,471
And there are others.

34
00:01:52,471 --> 00:01:54,971
None of them publishes a blueprint.

35
00:01:54,971 --> 00:02:00,208
But between their engineers' public talks and blog posts, exchange specifications,

36
00:02:00,208 --> 00:02:04,983
regulatory filings, a full day of real exchange data, and some experiments

37
00:02:04,983 --> 00:02:09,671
on this very computer, we can rebuild the machine they all converge on, piece

38
00:02:09,671 --> 00:02:13,496
by piece, and measure every piece.

39
00:02:13,650 --> 00:02:15,725
Here is that machine, as a loop.

40
00:02:15,725 --> 00:02:19,162
The exchange broadcasts every change to its order book.

41
00:02:19,162 --> 00:02:22,787
A feed handler decodes the messages, and rebuilds the book.

42
00:02:22,787 --> 00:02:26,650
The strategy estimates what the stock is really worth, decides where to buy

43
00:02:26,650 --> 00:02:30,500
and sell, passes every order through risk checks, and sends it back to the

44
00:02:30,500 --> 00:02:31,712
exchange.

45
00:02:31,712 --> 00:02:35,250
The time for one trip around this loop is called tick-to-trade, and at the

46
00:02:35,250 --> 00:02:38,100
top firms, it's measured in nanoseconds.

47
00:02:38,100 --> 00:02:39,900
We'll build it in five parts.

48
00:02:51,073 --> 00:02:53,898
This is what the market looks like to a trading machine.

49
00:02:53,898 --> 00:02:58,398
It's Nasdaq's order book for Nvidia, at 10:30 that morning, rebuilt from the

50
00:02:58,398 --> 00:03:00,461
exchange's own data feed.

51
00:03:00,461 --> 00:03:02,573
Every block is one real order.

52
00:03:02,573 --> 00:03:04,961
Its width grows with the number of shares.

53
00:03:04,961 --> 00:03:06,398
Below the middle are bids:

54
00:03:06,398 --> 00:03:07,961
orders waiting to buy.

55
00:03:07,961 --> 00:03:09,098
Above are asks:

56
00:03:09,098 --> 00:03:10,698
orders waiting to sell.

57
00:03:10,698 --> 00:03:14,611
At each price the orders wait in a queue, and the queue is served first come,

58
00:03:14,611 --> 00:03:16,307
first served.

59
00:03:16,307 --> 00:03:23,044
The best bid is 183.85 dollars, the best ask 183.86.

60
00:03:23,044 --> 00:03:25,119
The gap between them is the spread:

61
00:03:25,119 --> 00:03:26,194
one cent.

62
00:03:26,194 --> 00:03:28,369
Halfway between is the mid-price.

63
00:03:28,369 --> 00:03:34,432
Over the whole day Nvidia's spread on Nasdaq averaged 1.1 cents, on a 183-dollar

64
00:03:34,432 --> 00:03:35,369
stock.

65
00:03:35,369 --> 00:03:38,340
That's about six thousandths of a percent.

66
00:03:38,340 --> 00:03:41,590
A market maker's job is to always stand on both sides:

67
00:03:41,590 --> 00:03:44,215
willing to buy at the bid and to sell at the ask.

68
00:03:44,215 --> 00:03:47,565
Its orders join the back of the queues, like everyone else's.

69
00:03:47,565 --> 00:03:51,228
When a seller arrives, the bid queue is filled from the front, until it reaches

70
00:03:51,228 --> 00:03:51,815
us:

71
00:03:51,815 --> 00:03:55,340
we buy 100 shares at 183.85.

72
00:03:55,340 --> 00:03:58,340
Later a buyer arrives and works through the ask queue:

73
00:03:58,340 --> 00:04:02,078
we sell 100 shares at 183.86.

74
00:04:02,078 --> 00:04:05,165
Bought at 85, sold at 86.

75
00:04:05,165 --> 00:04:09,278
One cent per share, a dollar in total, for providing liquidity:

76
00:04:09,278 --> 00:04:13,040
for being there when someone wanted to trade.

77
00:04:13,665 --> 00:04:17,990
If every trader were like those two, market making would be easy money.

78
00:04:17,990 --> 00:04:22,040
It isn't, because some of the people trading with you know something you don't.

79
00:04:22,040 --> 00:04:24,190
Most of the flow is uninformed:

80
00:04:24,190 --> 00:04:28,415
people buying and selling for their own reasons, at random as far as the price

81
00:04:28,415 --> 00:04:29,765
is concerned.

82
00:04:29,765 --> 00:04:34,340
Against them, the market maker earns half the spread on every trade.

83
00:04:34,340 --> 00:04:38,540
But now and then, someone buys from you just before the price jumps, because

84
00:04:38,540 --> 00:04:42,890
they saw it coming, perhaps because their information arrived a few microseconds

85
00:04:42,890 --> 00:04:44,253
before yours.

86
00:04:44,253 --> 00:04:49,078
That one trade costs three and a half cents a share, seven times what an uninformed

87
00:04:49,078 --> 00:04:50,773
trade earns.

88
00:04:50,773 --> 00:04:55,261
In 1985, Lawrence Glosten and Paul Milgrom turned this into a theory of why

89
00:04:55,261 --> 00:04:56,948
spreads exist at all.

90
00:04:56,948 --> 00:05:01,198
If a fraction pi of the traders are informed, and they move the price by delta,

91
00:05:01,198 --> 00:05:04,548
the market maker breaks even only if what it earns from the many pays for

92
00:05:04,548 --> 00:05:06,136
what it loses to the few:

93
00:05:06,136 --> 00:05:09,073
half the spread must equal pi times delta.

94
00:05:09,073 --> 00:05:13,248
If one trader in eight knows the price is about to move four cents, the half-spread

95
00:05:13,248 --> 00:05:14,873
has to be half a cent.

96
00:05:14,873 --> 00:05:19,623
The spread is an insurance premium against being picked off.

97
00:05:20,407 --> 00:05:24,244
So the market maker's edge is a fraction of a cent per share, and it lives

98
00:05:24,244 --> 00:05:26,369
or dies on two abilities.

99
00:05:26,369 --> 00:05:27,319
Be right:

100
00:05:27,319 --> 00:05:31,107
know where the price is going, so the spread you charge covers the risk.

101
00:05:31,107 --> 00:05:32,182
And be fast:

102
00:05:32,182 --> 00:05:35,544
update your quotes before someone with fresher information can trade against

103
00:05:35,544 --> 00:05:36,307
them.

104
00:05:36,307 --> 00:05:40,257
With an edge that thin, the business only works at enormous volume, which

105
00:05:40,257 --> 00:05:44,507
means a machine making millions of decisions a day, each one right, and each

106
00:05:44,507 --> 00:05:45,457
one fast.

107
00:05:47,887 --> 00:05:49,687
Now the other side of every trade:

108
00:05:49,687 --> 00:05:50,887
the exchange.

109
00:05:50,887 --> 00:05:53,412
An exchange is at heart a machine for one job:

110
00:05:53,412 --> 00:05:55,750
deciding the order in which things happened.

111
00:05:55,750 --> 00:05:59,387
Thousands of connections from trading firms arrive through gateway machines,

112
00:05:59,387 --> 00:06:02,850
and every message from all of them is funneled into a single line, where a

113
00:06:02,850 --> 00:06:05,225
sequencer stamps it with the next number.

114
00:06:05,225 --> 00:06:09,287
Then a matching engine processes the messages one at a time, in exactly that

115
00:06:09,287 --> 00:06:11,212
order, on a single thread.

116
00:06:11,212 --> 00:06:12,800
One line, one thread:

117
00:06:12,800 --> 00:06:17,037
no locks, no ambiguity about who came first.

118
00:06:17,146 --> 00:06:20,258
The matching rule is called price-time priority.

119
00:06:20,258 --> 00:06:24,733
An incoming order to buy trades first against the cheapest offers, and among

120
00:06:24,733 --> 00:06:28,783
offers at the same price, against whichever arrived first.

121
00:06:28,783 --> 00:06:32,196
Whatever can't be filled rests in the book, at the back of the queue at its

122
00:06:32,196 --> 00:06:33,058
price:

123
00:06:33,058 --> 00:06:36,496
here it becomes the new best bid.

124
00:06:36,612 --> 00:06:40,675
Because the engine handles one event at a time, a single order can produce

125
00:06:40,675 --> 00:06:41,662
a flood.

126
00:06:41,662 --> 00:06:48,187
At 10:38 that morning, one order in Netflix traded against 4,076 resting orders

127
00:06:48,187 --> 00:06:49,275
at once.

128
00:06:49,275 --> 00:06:55,700
The feed reported it as 4,108 messages, every one stamped with the same nanosecond.

129
00:06:55,700 --> 00:06:59,512
To the matching engine it was one event.

130
00:06:59,671 --> 00:07:03,183
Then the engine has to tell everyone what happened, and fairly:

131
00:07:03,183 --> 00:07:05,571
everyone should learn it at the same time.

132
00:07:05,571 --> 00:07:09,371
The obvious way is a separate connection to each subscriber, but then the

133
00:07:09,371 --> 00:07:11,733
messages leave one after another.

134
00:07:11,733 --> 00:07:16,096
At ten gigabits per second, a thousand-byte message takes about a microsecond

135
00:07:16,096 --> 00:07:20,521
to put on the wire, so the six-hundredth subscriber hears the news six hundred

136
00:07:20,521 --> 00:07:22,583
microseconds after the first.

137
00:07:22,583 --> 00:07:26,529
That's the example Jane Street's Brian Nigito gives.

138
00:07:26,529 --> 00:07:30,129
So exchange data is sent with IP multicast:

139
00:07:30,129 --> 00:07:34,704
the engine sends each message once, and the network switches copy it in hardware

140
00:07:34,704 --> 00:07:37,254
to every subscriber at the same moment.

141
00:07:37,254 --> 00:07:42,604
It's unreliable, like all UDP, so every message carries a sequence number,

142
00:07:42,604 --> 00:07:48,279
and anyone who misses one asks a retransmission server to fill the gap.

143
00:07:48,462 --> 00:07:50,050
So what is in that stream?

144
00:07:50,050 --> 00:07:54,475
Here are the 803 million messages of the regular session on Nasdaq that day,

145
00:07:54,475 --> 00:07:56,100
as a thousand squares.

146
00:07:56,100 --> 00:07:57,975
Over a third are new orders.

147
00:07:57,975 --> 00:07:59,887
More than a quarter are replacements:

148
00:07:59,887 --> 00:08:02,300
an order's price or size changed.

149
00:08:02,300 --> 00:08:04,062
A third are cancellations.

150
00:08:04,062 --> 00:08:05,200
And trades?

151
00:08:05,200 --> 00:08:08,237
Two percent, about what Nigito estimates for the U.S.

152
00:08:08,237 --> 00:08:09,762
market as a whole.

153
00:08:09,762 --> 00:08:14,337
Ninety-seven percent of all the orders placed that day never traded at all.

154
00:08:14,337 --> 00:08:18,862
Most of the activity of a modern market is quoting, and re-quoting, and getting

155
00:08:18,862 --> 00:08:21,287
out of the way.

156
00:08:21,437 --> 00:08:23,987
That single numbered line buys something else:

157
00:08:23,987 --> 00:08:25,337
reliability.

158
00:08:25,337 --> 00:08:29,325
If every component is a deterministic function of the sequenced log, then

159
00:08:29,325 --> 00:08:33,637
a backup matching engine reading the same log computes exactly the same state

160
00:08:33,637 --> 00:08:38,087
as the primary, message for message, and so does the market data publisher,

161
00:08:38,087 --> 00:08:39,862
and the clearing reports.

162
00:08:39,862 --> 00:08:44,262
Lose any machine, and its replacement replays the log and catches up.

163
00:08:44,262 --> 00:08:47,087
It's called state machine replication.

164
00:08:47,087 --> 00:08:51,575
Jane Street built its own matching system, JX, this way:

165
00:08:51,575 --> 00:08:56,187
each component handles around half a million messages a second, with latencies

166
00:08:56,187 --> 00:08:59,987
of a few microseconds, and any one of them can be rebuilt from the log in

167
00:08:59,987 --> 00:09:01,250
under a minute.

168
00:09:01,250 --> 00:09:02,687
Remember this idea:

169
00:09:02,687 --> 00:09:05,462
the best trading systems are built the same way.

170
00:09:10,097 --> 00:09:13,222
Let's look at what actually arrives from the exchange.

171
00:09:13,222 --> 00:09:17,872
This is one real message from Nasdaq's data feed, exactly as it went out a

172
00:09:17,872 --> 00:09:20,635
millisecond after the opening bell that morning:

173
00:09:20,635 --> 00:09:22,210
thirty-six bytes.

174
00:09:22,210 --> 00:09:27,197
The format is called ITCH, and Nasdaq publishes its specification.

175
00:09:27,197 --> 00:09:28,097
Byte by byte:

176
00:09:28,097 --> 00:09:29,897
the first byte is the letter A:

177
00:09:29,897 --> 00:09:31,360
this is an Add Order.

178
00:09:31,360 --> 00:09:34,085
Two bytes give the stock's index for the day.

179
00:09:34,085 --> 00:09:35,822
Six bytes are a timestamp:

180
00:09:35,822 --> 00:09:40,347
the number of nanoseconds since midnight, which works out to 9:30 and 1.3

181
00:09:40,347 --> 00:09:41,747
milliseconds.

182
00:09:41,747 --> 00:09:46,197
Eight bytes are the order's reference number, one byte says buy or sell, four

183
00:09:46,197 --> 00:09:50,085
bytes the number of shares, eight the ticker, and four the price, in ten-thousandths

184
00:09:50,085 --> 00:09:51,422
of a dollar.

185
00:09:51,422 --> 00:09:52,472
Put together:

186
00:09:52,472 --> 00:09:58,147
someone wants to buy a hundred shares of Nvidia at 180 dollars and 35 cents,

187
00:09:58,147 --> 00:10:03,322
and from now on, that order will be known only by its number.

188
00:10:03,489 --> 00:10:05,502
Other messages are even shorter.

189
00:10:05,502 --> 00:10:08,014
A delete is nineteen bytes, and says only:

190
00:10:08,014 --> 00:10:10,089
remove order number such-and-such.

191
00:10:10,089 --> 00:10:13,802
An execution names the order that traded, and how many shares.

192
00:10:13,802 --> 00:10:17,739
A replace swaps an old order for a new one with a new price or size.

193
00:10:17,739 --> 00:10:19,277
Notice what's missing.

194
00:10:19,277 --> 00:10:22,902
A delete doesn't say the price, the side, or even the stock.

195
00:10:22,902 --> 00:10:26,264
To know what it means, you have to remember every order you have ever seen,

196
00:10:26,264 --> 00:10:27,177
all day.

197
00:10:27,177 --> 00:10:30,364
That's what the order book is for.

198
00:10:30,547 --> 00:10:35,010
Messages travel in UDP packets, a few at a time, behind a small header whose

199
00:10:35,010 --> 00:10:37,272
key field is a sequence number.

200
00:10:37,272 --> 00:10:39,460
Multicast is unreliable:

201
00:10:39,460 --> 00:10:41,035
packets can be lost.

202
00:10:41,035 --> 00:10:44,922
So exchanges send everything twice, over two separate networks, called the

203
00:10:44,922 --> 00:10:46,610
A and B feeds.

204
00:10:46,610 --> 00:10:49,622
The feed handler's first job is arbitration:

205
00:10:49,622 --> 00:10:53,622
for each sequence number, take whichever copy arrives first, and drop the

206
00:10:53,622 --> 00:10:54,922
duplicate.

207
00:10:54,922 --> 00:10:59,372
If a number is missing from both, there's a gap, and the book can't be trusted

208
00:10:59,372 --> 00:11:03,372
until it's filled from a retransmission server.

209
00:11:03,531 --> 00:11:05,481
And the volume is relentless.

210
00:11:05,481 --> 00:11:10,543
On an ordinary stretch of that day, about 34 thousand messages a second.

211
00:11:10,543 --> 00:11:15,781
In the busiest second, just after the closing bell, 1.75 million.

212
00:11:15,781 --> 00:11:17,868
And bursts arrive all at once:

213
00:11:17,868 --> 00:11:23,006
that single Netflix event was about 136 kilobytes, which takes over a hundred

214
00:11:23,006 --> 00:11:26,706
microseconds just to cross a ten gigabit wire.

215
00:11:26,706 --> 00:11:31,506
A feed handler has to swallow all of it, in order, without falling behind,

216
00:11:31,506 --> 00:11:35,581
because the moment it falls behind, every decision it makes is based on a

217
00:11:35,581 --> 00:11:36,881
picture of the past.

218
00:11:39,154 --> 00:11:42,292
So a feed handler must remember every live order.

219
00:11:42,292 --> 00:11:46,292
How you store them decides how fast you are, so here is the design almost

220
00:11:46,292 --> 00:11:48,129
everyone converges on.

221
00:11:48,129 --> 00:11:52,929
First, an array of price levels, indexed directly by the price in cents:

222
00:11:52,929 --> 00:11:56,442
finding a price is just arithmetic, no searching.

223
00:11:56,442 --> 00:12:00,029
Each level holds its orders as a first-in, first-out list:

224
00:12:00,029 --> 00:12:02,829
the front of the list is the front of the queue.

225
00:12:02,829 --> 00:12:07,204
Second, a hash table from each order's reference number to where that order

226
00:12:07,204 --> 00:12:11,954
lives, because cancels and executions name only the number.

227
00:12:11,954 --> 00:12:14,667
Watch the real add order from before arrive.

228
00:12:14,667 --> 00:12:16,942
Hash its number to find a slot in the map.

229
00:12:16,942 --> 00:12:20,867
Take a pre-allocated order record, fill it in, and link it onto the back of

230
00:12:20,867 --> 00:12:23,517
the 180.35 list.

231
00:12:23,517 --> 00:12:27,904
Update the level's total, and if the price beats the best bid, move the best-bid

232
00:12:27,904 --> 00:12:28,892
marker.

233
00:12:28,892 --> 00:12:33,367
A handful of memory accesses, and no matter how many orders are in the book,

234
00:12:33,367 --> 00:12:35,596
it takes the same time.

235
00:12:35,596 --> 00:12:36,409
A delete:

236
00:12:36,409 --> 00:12:40,709
hash the number, follow the pointer straight to the order, and unlink it:

237
00:12:40,709 --> 00:12:44,371
its neighbors now point at each other, and the record goes back on a free

238
00:12:44,371 --> 00:12:45,971
list for reuse.

239
00:12:45,971 --> 00:12:50,021
Nothing is searched, and nothing is allocated from the operating system while

240
00:12:50,021 --> 00:12:52,271
trading.

241
00:12:52,429 --> 00:12:53,542
How fast is it?

242
00:12:53,542 --> 00:12:58,129
Here is every message of SPY, the most traded ETF in the world, for the whole

243
00:12:58,129 --> 00:12:59,454
day on Nasdaq:

244
00:12:59,454 --> 00:13:03,129
almost fifteen million of them, through two implementations of exactly this

245
00:13:03,129 --> 00:13:04,117
design.

246
00:13:04,117 --> 00:13:07,842
In Python, about 780 nanoseconds a message.

247
00:13:07,842 --> 00:13:09,929
In C, about 38.

248
00:13:09,929 --> 00:13:13,704
And touching all the memory in advance, so the operating system never has

249
00:13:13,704 --> 00:13:19,442
to stop the program to hand it a fresh page, removes 6,819 pauses and brings

250
00:13:19,442 --> 00:13:21,179
it to 35.

251
00:13:21,179 --> 00:13:26,229
The whole day of SPY, rebuilt in about half a second.

252
00:13:26,338 --> 00:13:29,025
But averages hide the thing that matters most.

253
00:13:29,025 --> 00:13:32,838
Timing every update individually, the typical one takes well under a hundred

254
00:13:32,838 --> 00:13:37,175
nanoseconds, and ninety-nine in a hundred finish within a few hundred.

255
00:13:37,175 --> 00:13:40,988
But one in ten thousand takes more than ten microseconds, a hundred times

256
00:13:40,988 --> 00:13:43,975
longer, and the code had nothing to do with it:

257
00:13:43,975 --> 00:13:48,013
something else, the operating system or the virtual machine underneath it,

258
00:13:48,013 --> 00:13:49,725
interrupted the program.

259
00:13:49,725 --> 00:13:54,013
Those rare stalls tend to arrive exactly when the market is busiest.

260
00:13:54,013 --> 00:13:55,313
We'll come back to them.

261
00:13:59,961 --> 00:14:02,174
Why does any of this have to be so fast?

262
00:14:02,174 --> 00:14:04,399
Here's the clearest example I know.

263
00:14:04,399 --> 00:14:10,811
SPY, IVV and VOO are three exchange-traded funds run by three different companies,

264
00:14:10,811 --> 00:14:13,336
and all three hold exactly the same thing:

265
00:14:13,336 --> 00:14:16,799
the 500 stocks of the S&P 500.

266
00:14:16,799 --> 00:14:21,524
Over the day, measured by their prices on Nasdaq, they move in perfect lockstep,

267
00:14:21,524 --> 00:14:23,961
as they must.

268
00:14:24,170 --> 00:14:26,320
Now zoom in to a single second.

269
00:14:26,320 --> 00:14:28,232
They don't move together at all.

270
00:14:28,232 --> 00:14:32,732
Each fund's price jumps at its own moments, a few milliseconds apart.

271
00:14:32,732 --> 00:14:36,757
Information reaches each of them separately, through the separate decisions

272
00:14:36,757 --> 00:14:39,745
of the traders quoting them.

273
00:14:39,870 --> 00:14:41,207
We can measure this.

274
00:14:41,207 --> 00:14:45,045
Take the price changes of two of these funds over intervals of a given length,

275
00:14:45,045 --> 00:14:47,007
and compute their correlation.

276
00:14:47,007 --> 00:14:48,057
Over a minute:

277
00:14:48,057 --> 00:14:51,132
0.999, practically identical.

278
00:14:51,132 --> 00:14:52,032
Over a second:

279
00:14:52,032 --> 00:14:53,745
0.96.

280
00:14:53,745 --> 00:14:56,457
Over ten milliseconds, a coin flip's worth:

281
00:14:56,457 --> 00:14:57,482
one half.

282
00:14:57,482 --> 00:15:00,295
Over one millisecond, almost nothing.

283
00:15:00,295 --> 00:15:05,345
Economists Eric Budish, Peter Cramton and John Shim found the same collapse

284
00:15:05,345 --> 00:15:13,595
in 2011 data, between S&P 500 futures in Chicago and SPY in New York.

285
00:15:13,761 --> 00:15:15,824
That collapse is where the race lives.

286
00:15:15,824 --> 00:15:17,836
Suppose SPY ticks up.

287
00:15:17,836 --> 00:15:21,924
For a few microseconds, an offer to sell IVV at the old price is stale:

288
00:15:21,924 --> 00:15:22,886
too cheap.

289
00:15:22,886 --> 00:15:25,511
Somebody will try to buy it before it disappears.

290
00:15:25,511 --> 00:15:29,611
And the market maker who posted it will try to cancel it before that happens.

291
00:15:29,611 --> 00:15:31,236
Both see the same news;

292
00:15:31,236 --> 00:15:34,124
both send a message to the same matching engine.

293
00:15:34,124 --> 00:15:39,686
Whoever arrives first wins, and the one that loses, loses money.

294
00:15:39,828 --> 00:15:41,441
How often does this happen?

295
00:15:41,441 --> 00:15:45,566
Using the London Stock Exchange's own message logs, which record the losers

296
00:15:45,566 --> 00:15:49,978
of each race as well as the winners, Matteo Aquilina, Eric Budish and Peter

297
00:15:49,978 --> 00:15:54,316
O'Neill found that a typical race lasts five to ten microseconds.

298
00:15:54,316 --> 00:15:58,041
There's about one a minute in every major stock, races account for a fifth

299
00:15:58,041 --> 00:16:02,178
of all trading, and six firms are on one side or the other of more than eighty

300
00:16:02,178 --> 00:16:03,466
percent of them.

301
00:16:03,466 --> 00:16:08,653
In global stock markets, about five billion dollars a year.

302
00:16:08,795 --> 00:16:11,345
And you can see the race in Nasdaq's data.

303
00:16:11,345 --> 00:16:14,970
For every trade that day, in every stock, here is the time until the next

304
00:16:14,970 --> 00:16:16,907
order message in the same stock:

305
00:16:16,907 --> 00:16:19,632
a new order, a cancel, a change.

306
00:16:19,632 --> 00:16:23,445
Look at that spike, between twenty and twenty-five microseconds.

307
00:16:23,445 --> 00:16:25,957
That's the round trip of the fastest firms:

308
00:16:25,957 --> 00:16:30,057
Nasdaq publishes the trade, their machines receive it, decide, and send a

309
00:16:30,057 --> 00:16:34,570
response, and Nasdaq's own systems accept it and put it in line.

310
00:16:34,570 --> 00:16:38,670
Every reaction faster than that spike is a reaction to something other than

311
00:16:38,670 --> 00:16:39,907
this trade.

312
00:16:39,907 --> 00:16:42,145
The orders themselves are fleeting:

313
00:16:42,145 --> 00:16:46,307
14 percent of the orders canceled that day were canceled within a millisecond

314
00:16:46,307 --> 00:16:50,920
of being placed, and 42 percent within a tenth of a second, faster than a

315
00:16:50,920 --> 00:16:51,520
blink.

316
00:16:53,784 --> 00:16:57,434
Every race in this market is limited by one fact about the universe:

317
00:16:57,434 --> 00:17:00,272
information cannot travel faster than light.

318
00:17:00,272 --> 00:17:04,047
Here is the most valuable stretch of geography in American finance.

319
00:17:04,047 --> 00:17:08,859
In Aurora, Illinois, outside Chicago, sits the matching engine of the CME,

320
00:17:08,859 --> 00:17:11,684
where S&P 500 futures trade.

321
00:17:11,684 --> 00:17:16,209
And about seven hundred miles east, in New Jersey, sit the stock exchanges:

322
00:17:16,209 --> 00:17:20,334
Nasdaq in Carteret, the New York Stock Exchange in Mahwah, and many other

323
00:17:20,334 --> 00:17:22,705
venues around Secaucus.

324
00:17:22,705 --> 00:17:27,543
When futures move in Chicago, every quote on a related stock or ETF in New

325
00:17:27,543 --> 00:17:32,068
Jersey is suddenly out of date, so there's a race to carry the news east.

326
00:17:32,068 --> 00:17:37,530
The shortest path along the Earth's surface is about 1,178 kilometers.

327
00:17:37,530 --> 00:17:41,543
Light in a vacuum covers that in 3.93 milliseconds.

328
00:17:41,543 --> 00:17:42,855
That's the floor.

329
00:17:42,855 --> 00:17:47,468
No network, no algorithm, no amount of money will ever beat it.

330
00:17:47,468 --> 00:17:49,830
Light in optical fiber is slower.

331
00:17:49,830 --> 00:17:54,680
Glass has a refractive index of about 1.47, so even a perfectly straight fiber

332
00:17:54,680 --> 00:17:57,343
would take 5.77 milliseconds.

333
00:17:57,343 --> 00:18:00,280
And real fiber follows railways and highways.

334
00:18:00,280 --> 00:18:04,630
In 2010, a company called Spread Networks dug a new, straighter route through

335
00:18:04,630 --> 00:18:09,518
the Allegheny Mountains, 825 miles of cable, just to bring the round trip

336
00:18:09,518 --> 00:18:12,505
down to 13.33 milliseconds.

337
00:18:12,505 --> 00:18:15,526
Trading firms paid a fortune to lease it.

338
00:18:15,526 --> 00:18:18,214
Then the industry did something that sounds backwards:

339
00:18:18,214 --> 00:18:19,814
it went back to radio.

340
00:18:19,814 --> 00:18:23,926
Microwaves travel through air at almost exactly the speed of light, so firms

341
00:18:23,926 --> 00:18:28,301
built chains of towers, each able to see the next, as close to the great circle

342
00:18:28,301 --> 00:18:30,164
as the landscape allows.

343
00:18:30,164 --> 00:18:35,576
By 2016, one network was delivering data from Aurora to Carteret in 3.982

344
00:18:35,576 --> 00:18:41,276
milliseconds, just 53 microseconds, or 1.3 percent, slower than light in a

345
00:18:41,276 --> 00:18:42,776
vacuum.

346
00:18:42,776 --> 00:18:46,176
Let's race them, slowed down about sixteen hundred times.

347
00:18:46,176 --> 00:18:48,951
The same news leaves Aurora at the same instant.

348
00:18:48,951 --> 00:18:51,214
The microwave signal arrives first.

349
00:18:51,214 --> 00:18:55,339
A perfectly straight fiber would arrive 1.8 milliseconds later, and a real

350
00:18:55,339 --> 00:18:57,239
fiber route later still.

351
00:18:57,239 --> 00:19:01,626
In a market where races are decided by microseconds, that is an eternity.

352
00:19:01,626 --> 00:19:05,826
Microwave links carry very little data, and heavy rain can knock them out,

353
00:19:05,826 --> 00:19:09,964
so firms keep fiber as a backup and send only the most valuable bits through

354
00:19:09,964 --> 00:19:12,176
the air.

355
00:19:12,322 --> 00:19:15,210
The same logic plays out at the scale of meters.

356
00:19:15,210 --> 00:19:19,522
Exchanges rent space inside their own data centers, so trading servers can

357
00:19:19,522 --> 00:19:21,835
sit a few meters from the matching engine.

358
00:19:21,835 --> 00:19:23,810
That's called colocation.

359
00:19:23,810 --> 00:19:28,172
To keep it fair, exchanges give every colocated customer the same length of

360
00:19:28,172 --> 00:19:29,060
cable:

361
00:19:29,060 --> 00:19:33,447
one meter of fiber costs about five nanoseconds, so the customers closest

362
00:19:33,447 --> 00:19:36,547
to the exchange get extra fiber, coiled up.

363
00:19:36,547 --> 00:19:39,372
And yes, firms argue about this.

364
00:19:39,372 --> 00:19:45,622
In a 2024 letter to the SEC, a group of trading firms pressed Nasdaq to equalize

365
00:19:45,622 --> 00:19:50,835
latency across its whole Carteret campus, in a dispute where Nasdaq itself

366
00:19:50,835 --> 00:19:54,397
put one of the differences at 50 to 200 nanoseconds.

367
00:19:55,974 --> 00:19:59,899
Now let's go inside a single server, where the units shrink from milliseconds

368
00:19:59,899 --> 00:20:01,311
to nanoseconds.

369
00:20:01,311 --> 00:20:05,811
In one nanosecond, light travels thirty centimeters, about a foot.

370
00:20:05,811 --> 00:20:09,236
This machine's processor ticks every half nanosecond.

371
00:20:09,236 --> 00:20:13,024
Here's an experiment run on the computer that rendered this video:

372
00:20:13,024 --> 00:20:17,386
fetch memory from random addresses, each fetch depending on the last, and

373
00:20:17,386 --> 00:20:20,724
time each fetch as the amount of memory grows.

374
00:20:20,724 --> 00:20:24,699
While everything fits in the tiny first-level cache, a fetch takes about a

375
00:20:24,699 --> 00:20:26,436
nanosecond and a half.

376
00:20:26,436 --> 00:20:29,061
In the second-level cache, about five.

377
00:20:29,061 --> 00:20:31,924
In the big shared third level, fifty or more.

378
00:20:31,924 --> 00:20:35,799
And out in main memory, two to three hundred nanoseconds.

379
00:20:35,799 --> 00:20:40,461
Two hundred times slower than the fastest case, for the same line of code.

380
00:20:40,461 --> 00:20:42,874
Part of that cost isn't the data at all.

381
00:20:42,874 --> 00:20:47,024
Programs use virtual addresses, and the processor must translate each one

382
00:20:47,024 --> 00:20:51,299
into a physical address, with the help of a small cache of translations called

383
00:20:51,299 --> 00:20:52,349
the TLB.

384
00:20:52,349 --> 00:20:56,711
As engineers at Hudson River Trading explain on their tech blog, a miss in

385
00:20:56,711 --> 00:20:59,974
that cache means walking a tree of page tables:

386
00:20:59,974 --> 00:21:04,049
at least three dependent memory reads, or two hundred and ten nanoseconds

387
00:21:04,049 --> 00:21:07,411
in their example, before you even fetch your data.

388
00:21:07,411 --> 00:21:08,274
Their fix:

389
00:21:08,274 --> 00:21:13,574
huge pages, which let one translation cover 512 times more memory.

390
00:21:13,574 --> 00:21:16,099
Same experiment, with huge pages.

391
00:21:16,099 --> 00:21:21,261
Out in main memory, reads get about thirty percent faster.

392
00:21:21,369 --> 00:21:23,607
Put all the measurements on one ladder.

393
00:21:23,607 --> 00:21:25,819
A clock cycle, half a nanosecond.

394
00:21:25,819 --> 00:21:27,657
The three levels of cache.

395
00:21:27,657 --> 00:21:31,669
A system call, the cheapest possible request to the operating system:

396
00:21:31,669 --> 00:21:33,657
about a hundred nanoseconds.

397
00:21:33,657 --> 00:21:36,382
Handing a piece of memory from one core to another:

398
00:21:36,382 --> 00:21:37,919
about two hundred.

399
00:21:37,919 --> 00:21:38,907
Main memory:

400
00:21:38,907 --> 00:21:40,694
about three hundred.

401
00:21:40,694 --> 00:21:44,344
And sending one small message from one program to another through the operating

402
00:21:44,344 --> 00:21:45,919
system's network stack:

403
00:21:45,919 --> 00:21:49,757
almost three microseconds if the receiver is spinning, waiting for it, and

404
00:21:49,757 --> 00:21:52,632
fourteen if it's asleep and has to be woken up.

405
00:21:52,632 --> 00:21:54,157
To feel the proportions:

406
00:21:54,157 --> 00:21:57,794
if reading the first-level cache took one second, the sleeping receiver would

407
00:21:57,794 --> 00:21:59,744
take nearly three hours.

408
00:21:59,744 --> 00:22:03,644
Light gets from Chicago to New Jersey in four milliseconds, which on this

409
00:22:03,644 --> 00:22:05,044
scale is a month.

410
00:22:05,044 --> 00:22:07,207
And remember the market's reaction spike:

411
00:22:07,207 --> 00:22:09,144
twenty microseconds.

412
00:22:09,144 --> 00:22:13,282
Most of these rungs fit inside it many times over, but the bottom two eat

413
00:22:13,282 --> 00:22:14,219
a big piece.

414
00:22:16,423 --> 00:22:20,148
That bottom rung, the operating system, is the first thing a trading system

415
00:22:20,148 --> 00:22:21,348
gets rid of.

416
00:22:21,348 --> 00:22:25,148
Normally, when a packet arrives, the network card interrupts the processor,

417
00:22:25,148 --> 00:22:29,423
the kernel's driver and network stack process it, it waits in a socket buffer,

418
00:22:29,423 --> 00:22:33,398
and only when your program asks, through a system call, is it copied to you.

419
00:22:33,398 --> 00:22:36,660
If your program was asleep, it also has to be woken up.

420
00:22:36,660 --> 00:22:40,648
On this machine, that path costs about fourteen microseconds when the receiver

421
00:22:40,648 --> 00:22:43,398
sleeps, and three when it spins.

422
00:22:43,398 --> 00:22:47,473
Trading firms use network cards that write packets straight into a ring of

423
00:22:47,473 --> 00:22:52,160
memory inside the program, which watches that memory constantly.

424
00:22:52,160 --> 00:22:54,723
The kernel never touches the data.

425
00:22:54,723 --> 00:23:01,598
Brian Nigito puts a frame's trip from the wire into a program at under a microsecond.

426
00:23:01,773 --> 00:23:04,535
Inside the program, the same philosophy.

427
00:23:04,535 --> 00:23:07,048
Each job gets its own processor core:

428
00:23:07,048 --> 00:23:11,285
one decodes the feed, one runs the strategy, one sends orders.

429
00:23:11,285 --> 00:23:15,123
Each core is reserved, shielded from the operating system's other work, and

430
00:23:15,123 --> 00:23:16,285
never sleeps:

431
00:23:16,285 --> 00:23:20,785
it spins in a tight loop, checking for new work billions of times a second.

432
00:23:20,785 --> 00:23:23,560
The cores pass messages through ring buffers:

433
00:23:23,560 --> 00:23:26,698
a fixed circle of slots in shared memory.

434
00:23:26,698 --> 00:23:30,198
The producer writes into the next slot and advances its counter;

435
00:23:30,198 --> 00:23:33,548
the consumer reads behind it and advances its own.

436
00:23:33,548 --> 00:23:38,798
No locks, no system calls, and the memory is allocated once, at startup.

437
00:23:38,798 --> 00:23:39,848
Measured here:

438
00:23:39,848 --> 00:23:44,273
almost sixteen million messages a second, and a median hand-off of three hundred

439
00:23:44,273 --> 00:23:46,848
nanoseconds.

440
00:23:46,964 --> 00:23:49,602
But sharing memory between cores has a trap.

441
00:23:49,602 --> 00:23:53,989
Processors move memory around in sixty-four-byte blocks called cache lines,

442
00:23:53,989 --> 00:23:56,989
and only one core can own a line for writing at a time.

443
00:23:56,989 --> 00:24:00,852
Put two counters side by side, give one to each core, and let both increment

444
00:24:00,852 --> 00:24:03,077
their own, millions of times.

445
00:24:03,077 --> 00:24:06,727
They share nothing, logically, but the line they live on bounces between the

446
00:24:06,727 --> 00:24:08,677
two cores on every write.

447
00:24:08,677 --> 00:24:12,677
On this machine, that's about forty-six nanoseconds per increment instead

448
00:24:12,677 --> 00:24:13,627
of six:

449
00:24:13,627 --> 00:24:15,514
eight times slower.

450
00:24:15,514 --> 00:24:18,602
It's called false sharing, and the fix is just padding:

451
00:24:18,602 --> 00:24:22,614
keep each core's hot data on its own line.

452
00:24:22,781 --> 00:24:24,406
Another invisible cost:

453
00:24:24,406 --> 00:24:27,406
here's a loop that adds up the numbers above a threshold.

454
00:24:27,406 --> 00:24:32,006
On random data, it takes about three and three-quarter nanoseconds per number.

455
00:24:32,006 --> 00:24:34,968
On the same numbers, sorted, under half a nanosecond:

456
00:24:34,968 --> 00:24:36,606
eight times faster.

457
00:24:36,606 --> 00:24:40,306
The processor guesses which way each 'if' will go before it knows, and runs

458
00:24:40,306 --> 00:24:41,218
ahead.

459
00:24:41,218 --> 00:24:43,518
Sorted data makes the guess easy;

460
00:24:43,518 --> 00:24:47,581
random data makes it wrong half the time, and every wrong guess throws away

461
00:24:47,581 --> 00:24:48,706
work.

462
00:24:48,706 --> 00:24:53,031
So hot-path code is written to be predictable, and some firms even keep it

463
00:24:53,031 --> 00:24:57,781
warm, running fake decisions through it so the caches and predictors are ready

464
00:24:57,781 --> 00:25:00,681
when a real one comes.

465
00:25:00,823 --> 00:25:03,773
And then there's the noise that isn't your code at all.

466
00:25:03,773 --> 00:25:07,660
Here a reserved core on this machine did nothing but check the clock as fast

467
00:25:07,660 --> 00:25:11,160
as it could for ten seconds, and recorded every gap:

468
00:25:11,160 --> 00:25:14,460
every moment something else took the processor away from it.

469
00:25:14,460 --> 00:25:18,573
Tens of thousands of interruptions, eleven thousand of them longer than ten

470
00:25:18,573 --> 00:25:22,898
microseconds, the longest almost seven milliseconds.

471
00:25:22,898 --> 00:25:26,185
Those gaps are exactly the tail we saw in the ring buffer:

472
00:25:26,185 --> 00:25:30,135
a median hand-off of three hundred nanoseconds, but one in a hundred over

473
00:25:30,135 --> 00:25:31,960
a hundred microseconds.

474
00:25:31,960 --> 00:25:35,848
This computer is a virtual machine sharing hardware with strangers, which

475
00:25:35,848 --> 00:25:37,260
makes it noisy.

476
00:25:37,260 --> 00:25:40,810
Production trading servers are bare metal, with interrupts steered away from

477
00:25:40,810 --> 00:25:44,923
the trading cores, power saving switched off, and constant monitoring for

478
00:25:44,923 --> 00:25:49,573
exactly these spikes, because a race is lost in the tail, not the median.

479
00:25:52,102 --> 00:25:54,702
Software, however well tuned, has a floor:

480
00:25:54,702 --> 00:25:58,277
the whole packet has to arrive, cross into memory, and be picked up by the

481
00:25:58,277 --> 00:26:00,777
processor before any code can look at it.

482
00:26:00,777 --> 00:26:02,839
The fastest firms don't wait.

483
00:26:02,839 --> 00:26:06,739
Here is that real Nvidia order again, wrapped in the headers it travels in:

484
00:26:06,739 --> 00:26:08,402
104 bytes.

485
00:26:08,402 --> 00:26:12,752
On a ten-gigabit link, a byte arrives every eight tenths of a nanosecond,

486
00:26:12,752 --> 00:26:17,214
so the frame takes 83 nanoseconds to arrive, and the field that matters most,

487
00:26:17,214 --> 00:26:20,702
the price, comes almost last.

488
00:26:20,852 --> 00:26:24,327
An FPGA is a chip full of programmable logic:

489
00:26:24,327 --> 00:26:27,389
instead of running instructions, you lay out a circuit.

490
00:26:27,389 --> 00:26:29,639
A trading firm lays out a pipeline:

491
00:26:29,639 --> 00:26:33,702
decode the network headers, check the sequence number, parse the message,

492
00:26:33,702 --> 00:26:36,989
compare it against a table of triggers, send an order.

493
00:26:36,989 --> 00:26:42,139
The bytes stream in, eight at a time, one chunk every 6.4 nanoseconds, and

494
00:26:42,139 --> 00:26:46,627
each stage works on its piece of the frame while the rest is still arriving.

495
00:26:46,627 --> 00:26:49,552
And the reply can start before the input ends:

496
00:26:49,552 --> 00:26:53,677
the outgoing order's headers are prepared in advance, so the circuit starts

497
00:26:53,677 --> 00:26:57,977
sending them while the price is still on its way in, and fills in the decision

498
00:26:57,977 --> 00:27:03,302
at the last moment, or aborts the frame if the answer is no.

499
00:27:03,452 --> 00:27:04,977
How fast can this be?

500
00:27:04,977 --> 00:27:09,389
There's an industry benchmark for exactly this, called STAC-T0.

501
00:27:09,389 --> 00:27:16,077
The record, set in 2024 with an AMD FPGA card and Exegy's design, is a minimum

502
00:27:16,077 --> 00:27:20,464
of 13.9 nanoseconds from the last bit of the market data to the first bit

503
00:27:20,464 --> 00:27:21,639
of the order.

504
00:27:21,639 --> 00:27:26,227
In that time, light travels about four meters.

505
00:27:26,360 --> 00:27:28,473
But a circuit can't think very hard.

506
00:27:28,473 --> 00:27:30,048
So the work is split.

507
00:27:30,048 --> 00:27:31,673
Software is the brain:

508
00:27:31,673 --> 00:27:36,310
it works out fair values, risk limits and strategy, at its own pace, and keeps

509
00:27:36,310 --> 00:27:39,523
rewriting tables of instructions inside the hardware:

510
00:27:39,523 --> 00:27:43,248
if the price in this stock crosses this level, send that order.

511
00:27:43,248 --> 00:27:45,748
The FPGA is the reflex:

512
00:27:45,748 --> 00:27:50,710
it watches every packet and fires the pre-planned response in nanoseconds.

513
00:27:50,710 --> 00:27:56,423
Jane Street designs its FPGAs in Hardcaml, an open-source hardware language

514
00:27:56,423 --> 00:28:01,710
it built inside OCaml, the programming language it uses for nearly everything.

515
00:28:01,710 --> 00:28:04,660
And the same thinking reaches the network itself:

516
00:28:04,660 --> 00:28:09,110
special layer-1 switches that copy a signal in a few nanoseconds instead of

517
00:28:09,110 --> 00:28:09,960
hundreds.

518
00:28:14,616 --> 00:28:17,241
Speed only matters if you know what to do with it.

519
00:28:17,241 --> 00:28:19,441
The other half of the machine is the brain:

520
00:28:19,441 --> 00:28:23,816
an estimate, updated on every message, of what the stock is really worth.

521
00:28:23,816 --> 00:28:26,029
Start with the simplest clue there is.

522
00:28:26,029 --> 00:28:29,566
Here a thousand shares are waiting to buy at the bid, and only a hundred are

523
00:28:29,566 --> 00:28:31,054
offered at the ask.

524
00:28:31,054 --> 00:28:33,616
Which way is the price more likely to move?

525
00:28:33,616 --> 00:28:36,379
The thin side is the one that's about to run out:

526
00:28:36,379 --> 00:28:41,291
one modest buyer clears the ask, and the price ticks up.

527
00:28:41,441 --> 00:28:43,429
Let's test that on the real data.

528
00:28:43,429 --> 00:28:46,779
Define the imbalance as the fraction of the shares waiting at the best bid

529
00:28:46,779 --> 00:28:49,179
and the best ask that are on the bid side.

530
00:28:49,179 --> 00:28:52,979
Then, for every moment of the day when the spread was one cent, check which

531
00:28:52,979 --> 00:28:55,154
way the mid-price moved next.

532
00:28:55,154 --> 00:28:57,766
The relationship is almost a straight line.

533
00:28:57,766 --> 00:29:01,966
When nearly all the waiting shares are on the bid, SPY's next move was up

534
00:29:01,966 --> 00:29:03,841
85 percent of the time;

535
00:29:03,841 --> 00:29:08,091
when they're nearly all on the ask, down 85 percent of the time.

536
00:29:08,091 --> 00:29:12,504
Free information, sitting in plain sight in the order book, for anyone fast

537
00:29:12,504 --> 00:29:15,066
enough to use it.

538
00:29:15,233 --> 00:29:17,995
That gives us a first estimate of fair value.

539
00:29:17,995 --> 00:29:21,858
Instead of the plain mid-price, weight each side by the imbalance:

540
00:29:21,858 --> 00:29:27,120
with a thousand shares bid and a hundred offered, the imbalance is 0.91, and

541
00:29:27,120 --> 00:29:31,270
the fair price sits not halfway between bid and ask, but most of the way toward

542
00:29:31,270 --> 00:29:32,445
the ask.

543
00:29:32,445 --> 00:29:34,683
Researchers have refined this idea;

544
00:29:34,683 --> 00:29:39,158
Sasha Stoikov's version is called the microprice.

545
00:29:39,270 --> 00:29:41,308
But it's a fleeting kind of knowledge.

546
00:29:41,308 --> 00:29:44,895
Here's how strongly the imbalance predicts the price change over the next

547
00:29:44,895 --> 00:29:47,470
millisecond, second, or minute.

548
00:29:47,470 --> 00:29:51,120
It's strongest about fifty milliseconds ahead, and a minute ahead it's worth

549
00:29:51,120 --> 00:29:52,058
nothing.

550
00:29:52,058 --> 00:29:55,783
Whatever it knows, the market learns almost immediately.

551
00:29:55,783 --> 00:30:00,270
A signal like this is only worth having if you can act on it within milliseconds,

552
00:30:00,270 --> 00:30:04,895
which is why the brain and the pipe have to be built together.

553
00:30:05,095 --> 00:30:08,345
A real fair-value model combines many such clues.

554
00:30:08,345 --> 00:30:14,245
The stock's own order book, S&P 500 futures, arriving from Chicago by microwave,

555
00:30:14,245 --> 00:30:19,095
the same stock's quotes on a dozen other exchanges, related stocks and funds:

556
00:30:19,095 --> 00:30:23,345
an ETF and the hundreds of stocks inside it must agree, and keeping them in

557
00:30:23,345 --> 00:30:26,670
line has long been one of Jane Street's specialties.

558
00:30:26,670 --> 00:30:28,820
Options, and news.

559
00:30:28,820 --> 00:30:33,245
The models range from simple weighted averages to machine learning trained

560
00:30:33,245 --> 00:30:37,670
on years of data, which is why these firms run some of the largest computing

561
00:30:37,670 --> 00:30:39,470
clusters in finance.

562
00:30:39,470 --> 00:30:43,508
But every one of them produces the same thing, many times a second:

563
00:30:43,508 --> 00:30:45,920
a number, and how sure we are of it.

564
00:30:48,089 --> 00:30:51,664
With a fair value in hand, the market maker has to decide exactly where to

565
00:30:51,664 --> 00:30:52,514
quote.

566
00:30:52,514 --> 00:30:54,464
And there's a complication we skipped:

567
00:30:54,464 --> 00:30:55,577
inventory.

568
00:30:55,577 --> 00:30:58,627
Every time a seller hits your bid, you own more stock.

569
00:30:58,627 --> 00:31:02,327
Get hit five times in a row and you're long five hundred shares.

570
00:31:02,327 --> 00:31:05,414
Now every cent the price falls costs you five dollars:

571
00:31:05,414 --> 00:31:10,077
the profit from ten round trips at a half-cent each, gone in a single tick.

572
00:31:10,077 --> 00:31:14,764
A market maker wants to be a middleman, not an investor.

573
00:31:14,914 --> 00:31:19,589
In 2008, Marco Avellaneda and Sasha Stoikov worked out the optimal answer

574
00:31:19,589 --> 00:31:21,889
for a simple model of this problem.

575
00:31:21,889 --> 00:31:23,902
Don't center your quotes on the fair value s.

576
00:31:23,902 --> 00:31:28,439
Center them on a reservation price r, shifted against your inventory q:

577
00:31:28,439 --> 00:31:31,102
the more you own, the lower you go.

578
00:31:31,102 --> 00:31:35,414
The shift grows with your risk aversion, gamma, with the volatility, sigma

579
00:31:35,414 --> 00:31:39,439
squared, and with how long you expect to be stuck holding it.

580
00:31:39,439 --> 00:31:41,864
And the width of the spread has two parts:

581
00:31:41,864 --> 00:31:45,852
the same risk term, plus a term that depends on how quickly the chance of

582
00:31:45,852 --> 00:31:50,289
a fill falls off as you quote farther from the mid.

583
00:31:50,448 --> 00:31:51,748
Here's what that does.

584
00:31:51,748 --> 00:31:55,723
With no inventory, the quotes sit symmetrically around fair value.

585
00:31:55,723 --> 00:31:58,173
As you get longer, both quotes slide down:

586
00:31:58,173 --> 00:32:01,898
your ask becomes more attractive, so you're likelier to sell, and your bid

587
00:32:01,898 --> 00:32:04,960
less attractive, so you're less likely to buy more.

588
00:32:04,960 --> 00:32:06,673
Short, and they slide up.

589
00:32:06,673 --> 00:32:09,523
And when volatility rises, the spread widens:

590
00:32:09,523 --> 00:32:13,923
the same inventory is now more dangerous to hold.

591
00:32:14,081 --> 00:32:18,419
Here's the strategy running against a real minute of SPY, with its fills drawn

592
00:32:18,419 --> 00:32:20,906
at random the way the paper models them.

593
00:32:20,906 --> 00:32:23,606
The price swings through 44 cents.

594
00:32:23,606 --> 00:32:27,269
The quotes ride along with it, and lean against the inventory.

595
00:32:27,269 --> 00:32:31,356
The inventory wanders, but it never runs away, because in this model whether

596
00:32:31,356 --> 00:32:34,919
you get filled has nothing to do with where the price goes next.

597
00:32:34,919 --> 00:32:37,531
That's the skeleton of a real quoting engine.

598
00:32:37,531 --> 00:32:40,031
But real fills are not random.

599
00:32:40,031 --> 00:32:44,156
As we're about to see on real data, you tend to get filled exactly when the

600
00:32:44,156 --> 00:32:46,106
price is about to move against you.

601
00:32:47,830 --> 00:32:50,405
Now the hardest question in this whole business:

602
00:32:50,405 --> 00:32:52,580
does a strategy actually make money?

603
00:32:52,580 --> 00:32:56,030
You test it on history, but your orders weren't in the history.

604
00:32:56,030 --> 00:33:01,405
So take the real Nvidia bid queue at 10:30 that morning, six real orders,

605
00:33:01,405 --> 00:33:04,117
and put a virtual order of our own at the back.

606
00:33:04,117 --> 00:33:08,355
Because the feed reports every order individually, we can track exactly which

607
00:33:08,355 --> 00:33:12,355
orders are ahead of us, and replay what really happened to them.

608
00:33:12,355 --> 00:33:13,055
The replay:

609
00:33:13,055 --> 00:33:15,717
an order ahead of us is canceled, and we move up.

610
00:33:15,717 --> 00:33:17,967
Others join behind us and leave again.

611
00:33:17,967 --> 00:33:19,417
Another cancel ahead.

612
00:33:19,417 --> 00:33:23,755
Then, thirty-seven milliseconds in, a seller arrives and executes all four

613
00:33:23,755 --> 00:33:25,667
orders in front of us at once.

614
00:33:25,667 --> 00:33:26,892
We're at the front.

615
00:33:26,892 --> 00:33:31,330
A quarter of a millisecond later, a new order joins behind us and is executed

616
00:33:31,330 --> 00:33:32,542
immediately.

617
00:33:32,542 --> 00:33:35,205
In the real world that trade would have hit us first:

618
00:33:35,205 --> 00:33:40,005
we're filled, buying 100 shares at 183.85.

619
00:33:40,005 --> 00:33:44,730
And at that instant, the bid at 183.85 is gone.

620
00:33:44,730 --> 00:33:47,205
The price has just moved down through us.

621
00:33:47,205 --> 00:33:51,080
We bought exactly when the market was falling.

622
00:33:51,321 --> 00:33:53,434
Now run that idea all day long.

623
00:33:53,434 --> 00:33:55,534
A simple market maker on Nvidia:

624
00:33:55,534 --> 00:33:59,484
always join the best bid and the best ask with a hundred shares, re-join whenever

625
00:33:59,484 --> 00:34:03,484
they move, never hold more than five hundred shares either way, and assume

626
00:34:03,484 --> 00:34:07,184
every action takes ten microseconds to reach the exchange.

627
00:34:07,184 --> 00:34:11,421
Every one of its orders is a virtual order, queued behind the real ones.

628
00:34:11,421 --> 00:34:14,859
For each fill, measure the profit against the mid-price, at the moment of

629
00:34:14,859 --> 00:34:16,996
the fill and at later moments.

630
00:34:16,996 --> 00:34:18,996
This is called a markout.

631
00:34:18,996 --> 00:34:22,646
At the instant of the fill, we've earned a sliver of the spread.

632
00:34:22,646 --> 00:34:26,834
One millisecond later, the price has moved against us by more than that, and

633
00:34:26,834 --> 00:34:28,209
it stays there.

634
00:34:28,209 --> 00:34:34,784
Over 82,286 fills, the strategy loses about a quarter of a cent per share.

635
00:34:34,784 --> 00:34:37,109
That's adverse selection, measured:

636
00:34:37,109 --> 00:34:41,246
you get filled precisely when you least want to be.

637
00:34:41,421 --> 00:34:42,659
Now change one thing:

638
00:34:42,659 --> 00:34:45,471
how long our actions take to reach the exchange.

639
00:34:45,471 --> 00:34:49,046
From one microsecond up to a hundred, the loss barely changes.

640
00:34:49,046 --> 00:34:52,671
At one millisecond it's almost half again as bad, and at ten milliseconds,

641
00:34:52,671 --> 00:34:53,921
twice as bad:

642
00:34:53,921 --> 00:34:58,096
our quotes sit there stale, waiting for our own cancels to arrive, while faster

643
00:34:58,096 --> 00:34:59,796
traders pick them off.

644
00:34:59,796 --> 00:35:03,709
Add the imbalance signal, stepping aside whenever the queue on our side gets

645
00:35:03,709 --> 00:35:07,046
thin, and the loss shrinks, but doesn't go away.

646
00:35:07,046 --> 00:35:09,684
Real market makers have help we didn't model.

647
00:35:09,684 --> 00:35:13,746
Exchanges pay a rebate for posting orders that others trade against:

648
00:35:13,746 --> 00:35:18,721
on Nasdaq, around a fifth to a quarter of a cent a share for the busiest firms,

649
00:35:18,721 --> 00:35:22,396
which is just about the size of the loss of our fastest version.

650
00:35:22,396 --> 00:35:25,371
And the real edge is in everything else we left out:

651
00:35:25,371 --> 00:35:29,996
being first in line when a new price level opens, and signals from futures,

652
00:35:29,996 --> 00:35:34,146
other exchanges and related stocks that see the move coming.

653
00:35:34,146 --> 00:35:38,834
This is why a firm's simulator, which has to replay every queue honestly,

654
00:35:38,834 --> 00:35:41,146
is one of its most valuable systems.

655
00:35:45,712 --> 00:35:49,849
The last stage before an order leaves is the one that keeps the firm alive.

656
00:35:49,849 --> 00:35:52,237
Every order passes through risk checks:

657
00:35:52,237 --> 00:35:53,262
is it too big?

658
00:35:53,262 --> 00:35:55,812
Is its price far from where we think the stock is worth?

659
00:35:55,812 --> 00:35:58,237
Would it push our position past its limit?

660
00:35:58,237 --> 00:36:00,612
Are we sending too many orders too fast?

661
00:36:00,612 --> 00:36:02,687
Would it use more capital than we have?

662
00:36:02,687 --> 00:36:04,412
And is the kill switch on?

663
00:36:04,412 --> 00:36:08,399
Because they run on every order, they have to be fast, and some firms build

664
00:36:08,399 --> 00:36:11,337
them into the same hardware as the reflexes.

665
00:36:11,337 --> 00:36:14,037
And they have to be independent of the strategy:

666
00:36:14,037 --> 00:36:17,049
the strategy is the thing they protect us from.

667
00:36:17,049 --> 00:36:21,412
In the United States, any broker with direct access to the market has been

668
00:36:21,412 --> 00:36:26,712
required to have such automated controls since 2010.

669
00:36:26,845 --> 00:36:28,745
What happens without those checks?

670
00:36:28,745 --> 00:36:32,508
The most famous answer is Knight Capital, then one of the largest market makers

671
00:36:32,508 --> 00:36:35,195
in America, handling about a tenth of all U.S.

672
00:36:35,195 --> 00:36:36,508
stock trading.

673
00:36:36,508 --> 00:36:40,770
In late July 2012, Knight installed new code on the eight servers of its order

674
00:36:40,770 --> 00:36:41,683
router.

675
00:36:41,683 --> 00:36:43,370
A technician missed one.

676
00:36:43,370 --> 00:36:47,470
Server number eight still had a piece of old code called Power Peg, unused

677
00:36:47,470 --> 00:36:52,158
since 2003, and the new code reused a flag that, on that one server, switched

678
00:36:52,158 --> 00:36:54,120
Power Peg back on.

679
00:36:54,120 --> 00:36:58,120
On the morning of August 1st, starting at one minute past eight, Knight's

680
00:36:58,120 --> 00:37:02,708
systems sent ninety-seven automated e-mails mentioning Power Peg.

681
00:37:02,708 --> 00:37:07,520
They weren't designed as alerts, and nobody acted on them.

682
00:37:07,620 --> 00:37:13,295
At 9:30, server eight began working on 212 small customer orders, and for

683
00:37:13,295 --> 00:37:17,708
each one, it sent child orders to the market, again and again, because the

684
00:37:17,708 --> 00:37:21,370
code that counted how many shares had already been bought had been moved years

685
00:37:21,370 --> 00:37:22,545
earlier.

686
00:37:22,545 --> 00:37:23,895
Nothing stopped it.

687
00:37:23,895 --> 00:37:27,933
The engineers, trying to find the problem live, removed the new code from

688
00:37:27,933 --> 00:37:32,420
the seven good servers, which activated Power Peg on those too.

689
00:37:32,420 --> 00:37:34,120
In forty-five minutes:

690
00:37:34,120 --> 00:37:42,020
four million executions in 154 stocks, 397 million shares, a 3.5 billion dollar

691
00:37:42,020 --> 00:37:47,283
long position in eighty stocks, and a 3.15 billion dollar short position in

692
00:37:47,283 --> 00:37:48,995
seventy-four.

693
00:37:48,995 --> 00:37:54,195
Unwinding those positions cost Knight 460 million dollars, more than the firm

694
00:37:54,195 --> 00:37:56,058
could survive on its own.

695
00:37:56,058 --> 00:38:02,020
Within days it needed a rescue, and within a year it had been merged away.

696
00:38:02,153 --> 00:38:06,303
The SEC's findings are a checklist of everything in that risk gate.

697
00:38:06,303 --> 00:38:10,941
There was no automated, firm-wide capital limit connected to order entry.

698
00:38:10,941 --> 00:38:15,478
The account that filled up with unwanted shares had a two-million-dollar limit,

699
00:38:15,478 --> 00:38:18,816
but it wasn't wired to anything that could stop the orders.

700
00:38:18,816 --> 00:38:22,378
Position monitoring depended on people watching a screen.

701
00:38:22,378 --> 00:38:26,828
Nobody double-checked the deployment, and there was no plan for an incident.

702
00:38:26,828 --> 00:38:30,978
Today's top firms treat these as part of the trading system itself:

703
00:38:30,978 --> 00:38:36,216
limits on every order, kill switches, every fill reconciled against the exchange's

704
00:38:36,216 --> 00:38:40,841
own record, and deployments rehearsed and automated, so that a mistake costs

705
00:38:40,841 --> 00:38:42,653
seconds, not a company.

706
00:38:44,843 --> 00:38:46,643
Remember the exchange's trick:

707
00:38:46,643 --> 00:38:51,243
put every input in one numbered line, and make every component a deterministic

708
00:38:51,243 --> 00:38:52,981
function of that line.

709
00:38:52,981 --> 00:38:56,131
The best trading systems are built the same way.

710
00:38:56,131 --> 00:39:01,043
Every input, every market data packet, every acknowledgment from the exchange,

711
00:39:01,043 --> 00:39:05,343
even the ticks of the timer, is recorded in order, with a timestamp from the

712
00:39:05,343 --> 00:39:07,093
network hardware.

713
00:39:07,093 --> 00:39:11,493
The live system reads that stream, and so can a copy of exactly the same code,

714
00:39:11,493 --> 00:39:16,118
later, and it will make exactly the same decisions, message for message.

715
00:39:16,118 --> 00:39:20,318
That lets you explain any decision after the fact, test new code against last

716
00:39:20,318 --> 00:39:24,768
month's markets, and restart a crashed process by replaying its log.

717
00:39:24,768 --> 00:39:28,643
It's how Jane Street describes building its own systems, and it's why the

718
00:39:28,643 --> 00:39:34,768
simulator and the trading system are, as far as possible, the same program.

719
00:39:34,910 --> 00:39:39,360
To line up events from many machines, and to measure your own latency honestly,

720
00:39:39,360 --> 00:39:41,485
every clock has to agree.

721
00:39:41,485 --> 00:39:46,310
Left alone, computer clocks drift apart by microseconds every second.

722
00:39:46,310 --> 00:39:50,772
So firms discipline them all from a master clock locked to GPS satellites,

723
00:39:50,772 --> 00:39:55,435
using a network protocol called PTP, with timestamps applied in the network

724
00:39:55,435 --> 00:39:57,160
hardware itself.

725
00:39:57,160 --> 00:39:58,947
Regulators care too:

726
00:39:58,947 --> 00:40:02,910
in Europe, high-frequency traders must keep their clocks within a hundred

727
00:40:02,910 --> 00:40:08,560
microseconds of official time and record events to the microsecond.

728
00:40:09,310 --> 00:40:12,435
And then the whole machine sits inside a bigger loop, the one that makes it

729
00:40:12,435 --> 00:40:13,285
better.

730
00:40:13,285 --> 00:40:13,835
Data:

731
00:40:13,835 --> 00:40:16,597
every message from every venue, kept for years.

732
00:40:16,597 --> 00:40:17,385
Research:

733
00:40:17,385 --> 00:40:20,760
ideas and models, fitted on big computing clusters.

734
00:40:20,760 --> 00:40:21,735
Simulation:

735
00:40:21,735 --> 00:40:25,022
replaying the real queues, honestly, with your orders in them.

736
00:40:25,022 --> 00:40:27,110
A small live test, and measurement:

737
00:40:27,110 --> 00:40:30,547
did the fills, the markouts and the latency look the way the simulator said

738
00:40:30,547 --> 00:40:31,335
they would?

739
00:40:31,335 --> 00:40:34,710
If not, that difference is the most valuable data of all.

740
00:40:34,710 --> 00:40:37,010
The firms differ in style.

741
00:40:37,010 --> 00:40:41,585
Jane Street writes nearly everything in OCaml, a language whose strict types

742
00:40:41,585 --> 00:40:44,647
catch many mistakes before the code ever runs.

743
00:40:44,647 --> 00:40:49,322
Hudson River Trading puts C++ at the center of its trading systems and its

744
00:40:49,322 --> 00:40:53,635
research infrastructure, and says it has built one of the world's most advanced

745
00:40:53,635 --> 00:40:55,385
computing environments.

746
00:40:55,385 --> 00:40:58,085
But all of them run this loop, every day.

747
00:40:59,742 --> 00:41:03,755
So here's the whole machine again, with what we found at every stage.

748
00:41:03,755 --> 00:41:08,767
An exchange that puts every event in one line, on one thread, and multicasts

749
00:41:08,767 --> 00:41:11,005
the result to everyone at once.

750
00:41:11,005 --> 00:41:15,667
A feed handler that arbitrates two copies of the feed and bypasses the operating

751
00:41:15,667 --> 00:41:16,605
system.

752
00:41:16,605 --> 00:41:20,817
An order book that absorbs a message in tens of nanoseconds.

753
00:41:20,817 --> 00:41:24,817
A fair value built from clues that fade within a second, quotes that lean

754
00:41:24,817 --> 00:41:29,855
against inventory, risk checks on every single order, and, at the extreme,

755
00:41:29,855 --> 00:41:33,617
hardware that answers before the question has finished arriving.

756
00:41:33,617 --> 00:41:37,780
All of it racing the speed of light between Chicago and New Jersey, and other

757
00:41:37,780 --> 00:41:42,217
machines whose full round trip, on that ordinary Wednesday, took about twenty

758
00:41:42,217 --> 00:41:44,817
microseconds.

759
00:41:45,001 --> 00:41:46,038
To recap.

760
00:41:46,038 --> 00:41:49,751
A market maker earns fractions of a cent, and loses them to better-informed

761
00:41:49,751 --> 00:41:53,063
traders, so it must be both right and fast.

762
00:41:53,063 --> 00:41:57,326
Exchanges are single-threaded sequencers that broadcast by multicast, and

763
00:41:57,326 --> 00:42:00,538
nearly all of their traffic is quoting, not trading.

764
00:42:00,538 --> 00:42:04,651
Prices that move together over minutes move independently over milliseconds,

765
00:42:04,651 --> 00:42:06,676
and that's where the races happen.

766
00:42:06,676 --> 00:42:11,538
Distance is time, so firms bend radio waves along the shortest path and fight

767
00:42:11,538 --> 00:42:13,451
over meters of cable.

768
00:42:13,451 --> 00:42:18,001
Inside the box, they avoid the operating system, pin work to cores, and fight

769
00:42:18,001 --> 00:42:19,388
the slow tail.

770
00:42:19,388 --> 00:42:21,651
Hardware handles the reflexes.

771
00:42:21,651 --> 00:42:26,401
Fills are adversely selected, so everything is tested against real queues.

772
00:42:26,401 --> 00:42:28,426
And risk checks aren't paperwork:

773
00:42:28,426 --> 00:42:31,276
they're part of the machine.

774
00:42:31,409 --> 00:42:35,796
Every chart of market data in this video came from one real day of Nasdaq's

775
00:42:35,796 --> 00:42:40,271
feed, and every timing measurement from the computer that made the video.

776
00:42:40,271 --> 00:42:42,896
The sources are listed in the description.

777
00:42:42,896 --> 00:42:44,134
Thanks for watching.
